<h3>Typed Records in Your Code</h3>
<p>The endpoint returns cell text as strings. Type inference is a small step on your side, and keeping the raw text next to the parsed value means you can always fall back to it:</p>
<pre><code>import re

def to_value(text):
    """'$1,200' -&gt; 1200.0, '97.3%' -&gt; 0.973, 'N/A' -&gt; None, anything else unchanged."""
    t = text.strip()
    if t in ("", "N/A", "—", "-"):
        return None
    m = re.fullmatch(r"[$€£]?\s*(-?[\d,]*\.?\d+)\s*(%?)", t)
    if not m:
        return t
    value = float(m.group(1).replace(",", ""))
    return value / 100 if m.group(2) else value

records = []
for table in result["tables"]:
    headers = table["headers"] or [f"col_&#123;n&#125;" for n in range(table["column_count"])]
    for row in table["rows"]:
        records.append(&#123;h: &#123;"raw": cell, "value": to_value(cell)&#125;
                        for h, cell in zip(headers, row)&#125;)</code></pre>

<h2>9. Performance &amp; Benchmarks</h2>
<p>Methodology: 1,000 URLs from the top 10,000 websites (Tranco list), filtered to pages with at least one table. Ground truth: manual annotation of 200 pages by two independent reviewers (Cohen's κ = 0.91). Hardware: AWS c6i.4xlarge.</p>
<pre><code>Metric                   Simple BS4   Colspan-Aware BS4   Puppeteer
Structural fidelity      62.3%        78.1%               74.2%
Table discovery rate     68.4%        68.4%               86.7%
Avg extraction time      0.4s         0.5s                4.2s
Colspan/rowspan          ❌           ✅                  ❌
JS rendering             ❌           ❌                  ✅
Visual table detection   ❌           ❌                  ❌
Anti-bot handling        ❌           ❌                  Manual</code></pre>
<p><strong>Key findings:</strong> Simple parsers miss a third of tables entirely. Rendering alone isn't enough — Puppeteer found more tables (86.7%) but had lower structural fidelity (74.2%). Anti-bot handling is the hidden cost — DIY approaches hit Cloudflare or DataDome on 23% of commercial pages.</p>

<p>We have not published a fidelity benchmark for /v1/extract/tables. To evaluate it, run the same comparison on a hand-annotated sample of your own pages: fetch each page once, feed the identical HTML to each parser, and score the outputs against your annotations.</p>

<h2>10. Security Considerations</h2>
<p><strong>Authentication tokens:</strong> If extracting tables from pages behind a login, ensure tokens are transmitted over HTTPS and never logged in plaintext. Because /v1/extract/tables parses HTML you send, you can fetch logged-in pages with your own client and never share credentials with the API.</p>
<p><strong>Data retention:</strong> Ollagraph does not store the page content it fetches or the HTML you send for parsing; your usage history records the call itself.</p>
<p><strong>PII in tables:</strong> Tables often contain personal information. Ensure your extraction pipeline has appropriate access controls and data handling procedures.</p>
<p><strong>Respect robots.txt:</strong> Some sites explicitly disallow automated extraction. Check a site's policy before fetching — /v1/intel/robots returns it, and /v1/crawl respects robots.txt by default.</p>
<p><strong>Output validation:</strong> Extracted table data is untrusted input. Use parameterized queries for database insertion, sanitize cell values for web display, and validate types after extraction.</p>

<h2>11. Troubleshooting</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>"No tables found on page"</strong> (table_count: 0) — Search the HTML you sent for "&lt;table". If it is missing, the table may be built client-side (fetch it with /v1/scrape and <code>"wait_until": "networkidle0"</code> so client-side content has loaded), the fetch may have hit a block page, or the "table" is a div grid, which /v1/extract/tables does not detect — parse those in your own code. Also check that min_rows / min_columns are not filtering it out.</li>
  <li><strong>"Column count mismatch across rows"</strong> — Use a colspan/rowspan-aware parser and check the raw HTML for markup errors. /v1/extract/tables pads short rows so each table comes back rectangular, so look for empty trailing cells. Nested tables come back as separate entries in tables.</li>
  <li><strong>"Headers are empty or duplicated"</strong> — headers is null when the table has no &lt;thead&gt; and no all-&lt;th&gt; first row. Treat the first row as headers in your code, or specify which row contains headers.</li>
  <li><strong>"Layout tables in the output"</strong> — Raise min_rows and min_columns, and check whether the element is actually a data table versus a layout grid.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>"Page timed out during fetch"</strong> — Raise the /v1/scrape timeout (up to 60 seconds), or route through a proxy for sites that block datacenter traffic.</p>

<h2>12. Best Practices</h2>

<p>Render JavaScript for modern sites. If there's any chance the page uses client-side rendering, make sure the HTML you parse was captured after the table loaded. The extra seconds per page are worth the higher table discovery rate.</p>

<p>Plan for visual tables. CSS-based visual tables are common on pricing and comparison pages. /v1/extract/tables only parses &lt;table&gt; markup, so add a fallback for div grids unless you're certain all target pages use semantic &lt;table&gt; markup.</p>

<p>Filter out layout tables. Start with min_rows: 2 and min_columns: 2. Raise them for analytics dashboards (high precision); lower them for data mining (cast a wide net).</p>

<p>Validate output programmatically. Run automated checks on column count consistency, expected header names, data type patterns, and row count ranges.</p>

<p>Cache extracted tables. If extracting the same URLs on a schedule, cache results and only re-extract when the page changes. Hash the fetched HTML and skip the parse when the hash is unchanged.</p>

<p>Handle pagination. Many tables span multiple pages. Fetch each page (or use /v1/crawl to discover them), parse each one, and concatenate the rows in your code.</p>

<p>Monitor extraction quality. Track table counts, row counts, and header names over time. A sudden change often indicates a site redesign. Set up alerts when they drift outside expected ranges.</p>

<h2>13. Common Mistakes</h2>

<p>Assuming all tables use semantic HTML. You write a parser that queries &lt;table&gt; elements, test it on five pages where it works perfectly, then deploy and miss 30% of your target tables.</p>

<p>Ignoring colspan and rowspan. A single colspan="3" on a header cell causes every subsequent row to be misaligned unless the parser explicitly expands it. We've seen production pipelines where one colspan caused six months of misaligned pricing data.</p>

<p>Not rendering JavaScript. You fetch the HTML, find no tables, and conclude the page has no data. Quick test: disable JavaScript in your browser and see if the table still appears.</p>

<p>Treating all tables as data tables. Not every &lt;table&gt; element contains data. Some are layout tables, calendar widgets, or decorative elements. Filter with heuristics: minimum row count, presence of headers, cell content density.</p>

<p>Ignoring anti-bot protections. Your pipeline works for exactly 47 requests before getting blocked. Cloudflare, DataDome, and Akamai are everywhere. At scale, you need rotating proxies, browser fingerprinting, and request throttling.</p>

<p>Not validating output. You extract a table, it looks right in the first 10 rows, so you load it into your database. Three months later you discover columns were swapped on 30% of pages.</p>

<p>Overlooking nested tables. A table cell containing another table is rare but devastating. A naive parser flattens the nested table into the parent's row structure, producing garbage.</p>

