<h2>15. Enterprise / Cloud Deployment</h2>

<p>Concurrency: /v1/scrape/batch fetches up to 100 URLs per request with a concurrency setting. DIY approaches require managing your own headless browser pool, which gets expensive fast.</p>

<p>Queue management: For large-scale extraction, use a queue-based architecture. /v1/scrape/async and /v1/scrape/batch/async accept a webhook_url and call it when the fetch finishes; run each page's HTML through /v1/extract/tables in your webhook handler.</p>

<p>Monitoring: Track extraction success rates, table counts, and fetch latency. Set up alerts when success rates drop below 95% or table counts change sharply. A sudden change in fetch latency might indicate a site redesign.</p>

<p>Multi-tenant isolation: Create a separate API key per tenant (POST /v1/keys accepts a label, a project_tag, and an optional daily_cap_credits), so usage is tracked and capped per tenant.</p>

<p>Compliance: You can export your account data (GET /v1/me/export) or delete your account (POST /v1/me/delete). Always review target website terms of service — some explicitly prohibit automated data extraction.</p>

<h2>16. FAQs</h2>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p><strong>What's the difference between table extraction and general web scraping?</strong></p>
    <p>Table extraction is a specialized form of web scraping focused specifically on tabular data. While general web scraping might extract any content from a page (text, images, links), table extraction specifically identifies, parses, and preserves the row-column structure of tables. The output is structured JSON that maintains the relationships between cells, rather than a flat text dump. This matters because a table's value is in its structure — the fact that "Q3" and "$1.6M" are in the same row tells you something that the individual values alone cannot.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><strong>Can the Ollagraph API extract tables from PDFs?</strong></p>
    <p>The table extraction endpoint (/v1/extract/tables) is designed for HTML. For PDFs, use the PDF-to-Markdown endpoint (/v1/convert/pdf-to-markdown), which converts the PDF's text layer to Markdown. It does not OCR scanned PDFs — those come back flagged likely_scanned_pdf: true. PDF table extraction is a fundamentally different problem — PDFs don't have semantic table markup, so check how each table survives the conversion before relying on it.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><strong>How does the API handle tables with merged cells (colspan/rowspan)?</strong></p>
    <p>The API expands merged cells into their logical grid positions during the normalization stage. A cell with colspan="3" is treated as occupying three column positions, and the API replicates the cell's value across all three positions. Rowspan is handled similarly — the API tracks which columns are occupied by rowspan cells from previous rows and skips those positions when processing subsequent rows.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><strong>What happens if a page has no tables?</strong></p>
    <p>The API returns "status": "success" with an empty tables array and table_count: 0. A parsing failure returns "status": "error" instead, so you can tell the two apart. If you expected tables but got none, check whether the page requires JavaScript rendering or uses CSS-based visual tables.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>5. How accurate is the type inference?</h2>
<p>/v1/extract/tables does not infer types — every cell comes back as the text on the page, so type inference happens in your code (see the example above). The most common failure modes are ambiguous formats (e.g., "3/4" could be March 4th or 0.75) and mixed-type columns (e.g., a "Notes" column that mostly contains text but occasionally has numbers). Keep the raw text next to the parsed value so you can always fall back to the original.</p>

<h2>6. Can I extract tables from pages behind a login?</h2>
<p>Yes. Because /v1/extract/tables parses HTML you send, fetch the logged-in page with your own authenticated client and pass its HTML to the endpoint. Your credentials never go to the API.</p>

<h2>7. How does the API handle paginated tables?</h2>
<p>Fetch each page of the table yourself — follow the pagination links, or use /v1/scrape with actions for "Show more" buttons — then parse each page and concatenate the rows in your code. Extraction time grows with the number of pages.</p>

<h2>8. What's the maximum table size the API can handle?</h2>
<p>The practical limit is the size of the HTML you send rather than the table. For very large pages, trim the HTML down to the table's container before sending it, and split very large result sets into chunks in your own code.</p>

<h2>9. How do I handle tables that change frequently (e.g., live sports scores, stock prices)?</h2>
<p>/v1/extract/tables parses exactly what you send, so freshness comes from how often you fetch the page. Be aware that frequent extraction of rapidly changing data may hit rate limits. Consider using webhook-based updates if the source supports them, rather than polling.</p>

<h2>10. Can I extract multiple tables from a single page?</h2>
<p>Yes. The API extracts every &lt;table&gt; it finds and returns them as an array in page order. Each table has a caption (if one exists), headers, rows, row_count, and column_count. Use min_rows and min_columns to drop small layout tables, and filter by caption, headers, or position in post-processing.</p>

<h2>11. What happens if the table structure is malformed?</h2>
<p>The API parses with a forgiving HTML parser, so missing closing tags and similar malformations usually still parse, and short rows are padded so each table is rectangular. If the HTML cannot be parsed at all, the response has "status": "error". Validate column counts and header names in your code to catch tables that parsed but look wrong.</p>

<h2>12. How does the API compare to using an LLM for table extraction?</h2>
<p>LLMs can extract tables from raw HTML or rendered page screenshots, but they're slower, more expensive, and less reliable than a purpose-built table extraction API. In our tests, GPT-4 took an average of 12 seconds and $0.15 per table extraction, with 82% structural fidelity. A deterministic parser returns the same output for the same HTML every time. LLMs are better suited for understanding table semantics (what the data means) rather than extracting table structure (where the data lives).</p>

